import{Tt as e,at as t,rt as n,t as r}from"./chunks/framework.j55wycBY.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"2026-11-24 Detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"authorship":["ai"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reports/2026-11-24-detail.md","filePath":"reports/2026-11-24-detail.md"}`),a={name:`reports/2026-11-24-detail.md`};function o(r,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),n(`div`,{"data-pagefind-body":!0,"data-pagefind-meta":`date:1795521984000`},[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="_2026-11-24-detail" tabindex="-1">2026-11-24 Detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2026-11-24-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “2026-11-24 Detail”">​</a></h1><p><strong>Period:</strong> 2026-10-25–2026-11-24 <strong>Repository baseline:</strong> <code>69929a45c1878d22830b3417279c631ab69a883c</code> (exclusive; dated 2026-10-24) <strong>End commit on <code>main</code>:</strong> <code>db8c80a424b8833dc169a838e846b0c002d59ffe</code> (inclusive)</p><p>This period focused on billing reliability, Kubernetes deployment preparation, and API and tooling improvements. Local billing UAT covered several payment flows but left high-risk cases open; homelab deployment has not yet been verified end to end.</p><p>Repository evidence is limited to what landed on <code>main</code> in the commit range; pending PRs are referenced only as future work. A few commits in the range are dated 24 October. The billing UAT workbook and progress log are supplemental evidence published separately, and the homelab status includes information supplied by the project owner. The AI backend work merged in <code>6edb58723852</code> is excluded because it was reported earlier.</p><h2 id="_1-billing-reliability-and-contracts" tabindex="-1">1. Billing reliability and contracts <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-billing-reliability-and-contracts" aria-label="Permalink to “1. Billing reliability and contracts”">​</a></h2><h3 id="domain-and-transaction-improvements" tabindex="-1">Domain and transaction improvements <a class="header-anchor" href="#domain-and-transaction-improvements" aria-label="Permalink to “Domain and transaction improvements”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Refactored billing domain models and value objects, including money, payment, price, subscription, and refund-related types. The changes clarify ownership and invariants and reduce accidental mutation. Further cleanup removed unused contact-update behavior. See <a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/365" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#365</a>, <code>d78e47a3</code>, and <code>709bd76e</code>.</li><li>Introduced a <strong>transactional outbox</strong>: billing database writes and outgoing events are persisted in the same transaction, then forwarded asynchronously. Provider cancellation and refund submission are driven by outbox events. This reduces the risk of a committed state change without a corresponding event; it does not remove the need for retries and idempotency. See <code>d78e47a3</code> and the <a href="/notopia/services/billing/">billing documentation</a>.</li><li>Improved duplicate checkout handling under a workspace account lock and refund settlement handling, including not reopening settled refunds. Added or updated tests around billing domain behavior, provider interactions, and webhooks (<code>d78e47a3</code>).</li><li>Made billing features and limits explicit types across the contract and dependent services, rather than relying solely on generic string keys (<code>f758ec9c</code>, <code>18cca64c</code>).</li><li>Fixed Stripe customer-portal catalog configuration by specifying allowed update fields (<code>5196957d</code>), and improved sandbox customer descriptions (<code>775223ce</code>).</li></ul><h3 id="api-improvements" tabindex="-1">API improvements <a class="header-anchor" href="#api-improvements" aria-label="Permalink to “API improvements”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Changed <code>GET /billing/workspaces/{workspaceId}/subscription</code> to <code>GET /billing/workspaces/{workspaceId}</code> because the response contains both subscription and payer, either of which may be null. Updated nullable response fields and required-field declarations (<code>a780d8bb</code>). This is a route change for API consumers.</li><li>Expanded examples for plans, free workspace entitlements, and billing responses and clarified operation descriptions and webhook tagging (<code>ec85c290</code>, <code>a60d17ec</code>, <code>9e01d768</code>, <code>f6d2060c</code>, <code>2add6fca</code>).</li></ul><h3 id="manual-stripe-sandbox-uat" tabindex="-1">Manual Stripe sandbox UAT <a class="header-anchor" href="#manual-stripe-sandbox-uat" aria-label="Permalink to “Manual Stripe sandbox UAT”">​</a></h3><p>Manual testing was performed through Scalar and a Stripe sandbox. The <a href="https://gist.github.com/KevinNitroG/c777cd1c79d017c3ae9122fde8ebb753" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">billing UAT workbook and progress log</a> are published separately on GitHub Gist, not committed as report attachments. A complete billing UI was not available for this test; results do <strong>not</strong> establish an end-user UI flow or production readiness.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Recorded result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Catalog and free workspace</td><td>Pass: expected plans and free entitlements observed.</td></tr><tr><td>Plus monthly checkout</td><td>Pass: payment, active subscription, payer, and Plus entitlements observed.</td></tr><tr><td>Immediate cancellation and refund</td><td>Pass for the observed cancellation and refund behavior.</td></tr><tr><td>Plus monthly to Pro lifetime</td><td>Pass: Stripe confirmed cancellation and refund of the old subscription. Direct lifetime purchase from a free workspace was not tested.</td></tr><tr><td>Another recurring purchase; plan change; cancellation at period end</td><td>Partial, not full passes.</td></tr><tr><td>Declined initial payment</td><td>Partial; post-attempt entitlements response outstanding.</td></tr><tr><td>Lifetime cancellation</td><td>Local cancellation, free entitlements, and a successful refund were recorded; Stripe Dashboard confirmation and the outside-window variation remain outstanding.</td></tr><tr><td>Replayed webhook</td><td>Partial: a locally signed replay returned 204 and produced no duplicate local refund or subscription; full Stripe-side and API acceptance checks were not repeated.</td></tr><tr><td>Admin access</td><td>Admin reads and non-admin rejection observed; status capture and update/invalid-input cases incomplete.</td></tr><tr><td>Direct yearly purchase</td><td>Skipped.</td></tr><tr><td>Duplicate checkout, permission boundaries, delivery interruption/recovery</td><td>Deferred, blocked, or not tested; permission testing lacked a second workspace user.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The record notes one transient local DNS-related webhook delivery failure; the cause was not established. It also identifies sensitive headers, cookies, webhook payloads, and other sensitive data in logs, with a token exposed in diagnostic output. <strong>Follow up by revoking the affected session/token and fixing sensitive logging</strong>; the notes do not establish that this has already happened.</p><h2 id="_2-kubernetes-helm-and-homelab" tabindex="-1">2. Kubernetes, Helm, and homelab <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-kubernetes-helm-and-homelab" aria-label="Permalink to “2. Kubernetes, Helm, and homelab”">​</a></h2><h3 id="implemented" tabindex="-1">Implemented <a class="header-anchor" href="#implemented" aria-label="Permalink to “Implemented”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Added a shared Helm library and service charts for web, note, authorization, billing, document, and search-worker, plus an authenticated edge. A chart for the existing AI service was included in later deployment work. Charts include service-specific values, schemas, workload/service resources, optional autoscaling and disruption controls, migration or seed hooks where applicable, and automated chart checks. See <a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/370" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#370</a> (<code>72beeadf</code>) and <a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/379" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#379</a> (<code>8c90e9bb</code>).</li><li>Prepared applications for deployment: separated migration and mandatory-data initialization commands, added or improved health/readiness behavior, and adjusted deployment-related configuration. Search-worker readiness accounts for Kafka connectivity; document includes dependency readiness and a migration runner.</li><li>Prepared Flux-based homelab GitOps wiring for releases and infrastructure, including databases, Meilisearch, Valkey instances, and encrypted secret manifests (<a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/376" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#376</a>, <code>3af652e4</code>). Upgraded the <strong>Valkey Helm chart</strong> to <code>1.0.0</code> (<code>46201ed2</code>); this refers to the chart version, not a claim that the Valkey server is version 1.0.0.</li><li>Added Kubernetes CI and service-release automation, chart version checks, release tag scripts, and an initial development release (<code>8c90e9bb</code>, <code>a97d424b</code>). Improved test reporting and reduced duplicate CI runs (<code>c97d3dad</code>, <code>d1aaf760</code>, <code>7766cc6d</code>).</li><li>Prepared additional homelab values and secrets, plus RustFS and Stripe webhook setup documentation (<code>6e9f301f</code>, <code>4522f0b6</code>). Not all inputs are filled.</li></ul><h3 id="still-in-progress-deployment-gates" tabindex="-1">Still in progress / deployment gates <a class="header-anchor" href="#still-in-progress-deployment-gates" aria-label="Permalink to “Still in progress / deployment gates”">​</a></h3><p>The project owner reports that homelab secrets and environment values are being populated and RustFS is being configured. The application has <strong>not yet been deployed and verified end to end</strong> on the homelab.</p><p>Before treating the deployment as ready, the <a href="/notopia/developer/limitations/kubernetes-deployment.html">documented Kubernetes limitations</a> call for live verification of identity-header boundaries and direct-access prevention, authorization policy seeding and safe recovery, image permissions and filesystems, health and routing, and upgrades. Document replica event delivery also has <a href="/notopia/developer/limitations/document-replica-events.html">known limitations</a>. Rendered charts and unit tests are useful but cannot prove these live-cluster properties.</p><h2 id="_3-api-correctness-and-developer-authentication" tabindex="-1">3. API correctness and developer authentication <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-api-correctness-and-developer-authentication" aria-label="Permalink to “3. API correctness and developer authentication”">​</a></h2><ul><li>In <code>234f7efa</code>, introduced separate creation request schemas for folders, notes, and workspaces instead of reusing full resource/response schemas. The latter include server-generated, read-only fields that may be required in responses but should not be required from clients. The note API was <strong>already OpenAPI 3.1.2</strong> before this commit; this was a contract correction for 3.1 validation, not an upgrade from 3.0 to 3.1 in this reporting range.</li><li>OpenAPI 3.1 treats <code>readOnly</code> as a JSON Schema annotation and discusses applying <code>required</code> in the relevant request/response context; tooling can interpret this differently. Separate schemas make the input contract explicit and avoid relying on validator-specific behavior. Reference: <a href="https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.1.2.html#extended-validation-with-readonly-and-writeonly" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">OpenAPI 3.1.2 Schema Object: <code>readOnly</code> and <code>writeOnly</code></a>.</li><li>Enabled local Authentik fixture users and the API viewer client for password-grant testing (<code>a115e9d0</code>), complementing authorization-code and refresh-token configuration (<code>aba81097</code>). These fixture credentials and password grants are for local development; do not generalize them to production use.</li><li>Expanded OAuth default scopes for API exploration (<code>17cc93ac</code>). Updated Redocly CLI to <code>2.82.0</code> (<code>2e80d890</code>); the update did not require application code changes.</li></ul><h2 id="_4-toolchains-dependencies-and-build-quality" tabindex="-1">4. Toolchains, dependencies, and build quality <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-toolchains-dependencies-and-build-quality" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Toolchains, dependencies, and build quality”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Split mise into shared, dev, and CI environments and added per-environment lockfiles (<code>711a6384</code>, <code>201943ad</code>). The split avoids installing local-only tools in CI and vice versa; locked versions, checksums, and dependency graphs support repeatable installations across machines. See <a href="/notopia/developer/mise.html">mise tooling</a>.</li><li>Upgraded the gRPC/Protobuf toolchain (<a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/391" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#391</a>, <code>93c0910c</code>): Buf <code>1.65.0 → 1.75.0</code>, ts-proto <code>2.12.4 → 2.22.0</code>, Go gRPC <code>1.84.0 → 1.85.0</code>, <code>@bufbuild/protobuf</code> <code>2.11.0 → 2.17.0</code>, protobufjs <code>8.4 → 8.8</code>, and related validation dependencies. Regenerated TypeScript protobuf output and made proto lint cache sensitive to Buf upgrades. Later updated Go protovalidate to <code>1.4.1</code> (<code>a349cfe8</code>).</li><li>Removed <code>apps/document/inquire-shim.js</code>, not a generated proto stub (<a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/392" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#392</a>, <code>eabfdb45</code>). It had worked around protobufjs dynamic module loading in Rspack; the replacement no longer matched a dependency, and the service uses generated ts-proto package definitions instead of loading <code>.proto</code> files at runtime. The recorded investigation found byte-identical application bundles with and without the shim. See <a href="/notopia/developer/story/nestjs-bundling-otel.html">the bundling investigation</a> and <a href="/notopia/developer/story/nestjs-grpc-proto-loader.html">gRPC proto-loader investigation</a>.</li><li>Other notable updates include Node 26, Go 1.27.4, Nx 24.0.6, NestJS 11.2.11, TypeORM 1.1.3, Next.js 16.4.6, Vitest 5.1.1, Rspack 2.3.3, oapi-codegen 2.9.0, and OpenAPI Generator CLI 2.42.2. Infrastructure updates included PostgreSQL 18.7, Meilisearch 1.57, Grafana 13.3.1, Tempo 3, and updated Authentik, Redpanda, and RustFS images. These are selected examples, not an exhaustive dependency inventory.</li><li>Aligned Go and Node OpenTelemetry dependencies (<a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/380" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#380</a>); pinned <code>cron</code> to <code>4.4.0</code> for compatibility with <code>@nestjs/schedule</code> (<code>0a59ca58</code>). Enabled type-aware Oxlint and fixed identified issues (<code>891cecbe</code>, <code>00cd90e2</code>).</li></ul><h2 id="_5-documentation-and-other-improvements" tabindex="-1">5. Documentation and other improvements <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-documentation-and-other-improvements" aria-label="Permalink to “5. Documentation and other improvements”">​</a></h2><ul><li>Reorganized documentation by service and topic; expanded architecture, domain, data, flow, deployment, and API pages (<code>cf5840a8</code>, <code>7131befe</code>, <code>5cb49a7d</code>). Added generated gRPC reference documentation (<code>12bb02cf</code>).</li><li>Added validation and publication of documentation previews for pull requests (<code>251bab99</code>); fixed VitePress OpenAPI server-side rendering and documented the issue (<code>0df87429</code>, <code>bac0ff31</code>).</li><li>Fixed document collaboration storage behavior: <code>flushStore</code> waits for queued stores, and peer modification state is preserved on load (<code>c11d7019</code>, <code>2273ca38</code>).</li><li>Updated the workspace publication design (<code>fb4c5c38</code>, <code>c80f8eaa</code>). This is design work, not evidence that workspace publication was implemented during this period.</li></ul><h2 id="_6-future-work-and-dependencies" tabindex="-1">6. Future work and dependencies <a class="header-anchor" href="#_6-future-work-and-dependencies" aria-label="Permalink to “6. Future work and dependencies”">​</a></h2><h3 id="near-term" tabindex="-1">Near term <a class="header-anchor" href="#near-term" aria-label="Permalink to “Near term”">​</a></h3><ol><li>Finish RustFS and other homelab inputs, deploy the prepared releases, and run live security, startup, upgrade, and application-flow checks.</li><li>Resume prioritized billing UAT and add suitable automated integration coverage for duplicate charges, authorization, and webhook recovery. Resolve the logging and token/session security follow-up.</li><li><strong>NestJS 12:</strong> wait for a compatible stable Nx release and confirm its Nest peer ranges before upgrading. The 2026-11-21 plan records upstream Nx support merged, but not yet available in the stable release used at that snapshot. <a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/350" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#350</a> covers the Nest upgrade; <a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/396" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#396</a> is a Terminus maintenance update, not the core blocker. The plan identified a search-worker Kafka mock requiring adaptation because Nest 12 makes <code>createClient</code> asynchronous; this was a migration finding, not a completed fix.</li><li><strong>Native ESM:</strong> evaluate separately for document, AI, and search-worker. OpenTelemetry does not categorically prohibit ESM, but instrumentation must start before application imports and may require an ESM loader hook; Nest-specific instrumentation also has its own version-support limit. Align build output, telemetry preload, Docker and Kubernetes commands, migration/seed entrypoints, and verify actual logs, traces, startup, and shutdown. BlockNote compatibility is related but does not inherently block ESM or require an ESM application bundle. This is a plan, not an implemented migration. Source: <code>docs/superpowers/plans/2026-11-21-nestjs-12-native-esm-migration.md</code> (<code>7374de9f</code>).</li><li><strong>TypeORM extension:</strong> consider <a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/346" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#346</a>, including CLI and fixture validation. The plan records that extension 3.9.0 has an older TypeORM peer range while the project already resolves TypeORM 1.1.3.</li></ol><h3 id="longer-term" tabindex="-1">Longer term <a class="header-anchor" href="#longer-term" aria-label="Permalink to “Longer term”">​</a></h3><ul><li>Evaluate BlockNote <code>0.55 → 0.57</code> (<a href="https://github.com/notopia-uit/notopia/pull/238" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">#238</a>), including its ESM-only distribution, server-side DOM API changes, and Yjs compatibility. Investigate whether its version-history facilities can satisfy some of the current manually saved Yjs revision and rollback requirements; compatibility and the version in which the feature first appeared have <strong>not</strong> been verified. Examples for future review: <a href="https://www.blocknotejs.org/examples/collaboration/versioning-yjs14" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Yjs 14 versioning</a> and <a href="https://www.blocknotejs.org/examples/extensions/versioning" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">versioning extension</a>. Do not assume that adopting the feature or Yjs 14 is appropriate without further investigation.</li></ul>`,30)]])}var s=r(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};